uld start to use them.\nThis program was never designed to be perfect since the start, so in collaboration with the ECMA, there were new programs released: ECMAScript 2 in 1998 and ECMAScript 3 in 1999, which is the baseline for the current JavaScript."}, {"time": "73000", "image": "enpe7n6le03ob12re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Microsoft did not want to implement JavaScript in Internet Explorer, but in 2003, they gave up and accepted it.<br/>\n2005 was a very important year for programming languages.  Brendan Eich, JavaScript creator, and Mozilla rejoined ECMA and worked in a new programming language.  Macromedia and Adobe did the same. But, in 2007 and 2008, they realized that their projects were very different from JavaScript, and so they could not be converged.<br/>\nBack in 2005, Jesse James Garret established described different technologies, but JavaScript was the backbone of all. JavaScript allowed the creation of dynamic web applications.<br/>\nThe different parties involving programming languages met in Oslo in 2008, they came to an agreement, and the previously ECMAScript 5 was improved and called ECMAScript 5.<br/>\nThis is as far, the history of JavaScript, but there are many improvements that can be done, and it's going to be even better."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob12re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob12re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "video", "template_override": 1, "id": 2, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n6le03ob12re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}]}], "type": "scholar"}]}